No step needed something the design does not provide. The argument found no fault of the checker; it found that the model's observation is behind the checker's ([Obs-ref]), which was known, and it proposes one change to the model (@sec-frame-id).

= The frame property <sec-frame>

_Rewritten by `theory4`, after the second referee (@sec-referee2)._ A function's body is run in two ways. The checker runs it _in place_: a frame is pushed on the caller's state, each borrow parameter holds the caller's borrow, and the body's writes reach the caller's places through it. The model reads the function's _meaning_ off a run of the same body in the function's _own_ state: its captured values, and for each borrow parameter a fresh borrow whose loan sits in a _cell_, a binding below the body's frame that ends holding the parameter's final content. The frame property says the two runs agree: both fail, or both succeed with the same result and states that correspond. Every call that runs in place rests on it, and so do the other shapes in which the argument of @sec-twohalves uses it: a statement read at a call site, a match closed off as a block, a closure's value, a sealed program's re-run.

It has been stated five times and refuted five times, each time by a state the statement admitted and no run makes, or by a value compared through the renaming of labels that the run reads another way (D113, D127, D129, D201, D203); the invariant of the last paper proof was refuted too (`P2`, @sec-referee2, R4). This section states it again, reading every clause off the model's definitions on `origin/spine` at 0e76096ff (`Meaning/Run.lean` whole; `SState.lean`, `CallRef.lean`, `RunCongr.lean`'s `fixStep`, `fixBodyAt`, `fixG`; `FrameProp.lean`; `Steps/FrameFrag.lean`, `FrameRec.lean`, `FrameEnd.lean`, `FrameEndShape.lean`; the refutations `Strict/FrameLocCE.lean` and `Strict/FrameCellCE.lean`). In order: the objects (@sec-frame-rel); three changes to the model without which no statement covers the argument's uses (@sec-frame-model); the theorem, its hypotheses with the counterexample that forces each, and its uses as corollaries (@sec-frame-stmt); the invariant that replaces (P) (@sec-frame-inv); the proof, one lemma per operation on states and per clause of the run (@sec-frame-proof); the call in place, with the fixpoint equation (@sec-frame-fix); `Id` over a parameter (@sec-frame-id); the block lemma, a theorem of its own (@sec-frame-block); frames at a depth, the route if the model's closures are not changed (@sec-frame-depth); and what is proved and what is not (@sec-frame-found).

*Verdict, in short.* The statement (theorem FR below) survived every clause of the reference run I read it against. Its proof is argued on paper for every clause, assuming three changes to the model, one of which (M1) is the second referee's `G1` fault; `Id` over a parameter is reduced to a hypothesis on the call (resolved determination), which rests on N5. Not proved: the fixpoint equation of a closure formed by an untyped run (CloDesc), the place renaming the block lemma needs (PR), and, if the model's closures are not changed, a closure formed in the body. Of the argument's seven uses, two are corollaries (a call in place; a statement read at a call site), two become definitions once the model reads closures from their captures as the checker does (a closure's value at its forming; the parameter types at the start), two are the theorem's case with no caller (a sealed program's re-run, and the step from (II) to the theorem: the renaming lemma), and one is not an instance and is a theorem of its own (a match run directly, against the block it would close off as: N8). Two findings for the Lean lane: the clause "the renaming is monotone on the callee's labels" that docs/31 D208 proposes to carry is false at a state an accepted program reaches (`P4`), and is not needed; and `run_frame_obs` is false as stated, its hypothesis `CellsDetermined` being of the wrong form (@sec-frame-id). No fault of the checker was found.

Lemmas are marked #a-full (argued in full on paper here), #a-lean (named), #a-red[a named lemma], or #a-np (with what is missing).

== The objects <sec-frame-rel>

*A reference state* (`SState`). Frames of bindings and temporaries (`env`), oldest first. Each binding has a stored type (a code, which names a label when the binding holds a borrow), a declared reading and a _meaning_, a function from futures to values (`SVal`); a _future_ gives each label the final content of its borrow. Beside the frames: which position holds each label's borrow (`shape`; one holder per label, `Uniq`); where each label is lent (`lent`: _sites_, each a position, a path below it, and whether the label has its loan there or a fill); each label's content type and a member recorded when it was made (`heldTy`, `heldMem`); and the label counter (`nextLoan`). A label is _live_ when it has a holder (`liveLabels`). A _typed future_ of a state (`TypedFut`) agrees with the run's future off the live labels and gives each live label a whole member of its content type. A read is taken at the future that gives each live label its record (`typedFuture`).

*What the run does to states.* [End] of a label ℓ (`endLabel`, `Mean.semEnd` with the sites carried): the holder becomes _moved_; every other meaning reads ℓ as the holder's current meaning (a meaning $v$ becomes $psi |-> v(psi[ell := x(psi)])$, $x$ the holder's); and the sites of the borrows lent inside the holder's content move to ℓ's sites (`relend`). [Access] of a place (`access`): round by round, in label order, end every live label with a site on the path to the place, at it or inside it (`dataHit`; for a match's tag, on the path and at it, `tagHit`); it reads sites, never meanings. A borrow (`borrowPlace`): a fresh label from the counter, the place now reading the label's final content, one loan site, the content recorded. A write at a path (`setContent`): defined only if the path exists at every typed future. The whole-state test after a write or a borrow at a field path (`lentOk`, `StateOk`): every binding of every frame holds an open member of its type at every typed future. A call (`callOf`, `callOfL`): it never runs a body; it applies the callee's meaning along its type's code, takes the borrow arguments' holders, and makes every position of the state read each consumed label as the callee's final content for it, or, for a returned borrow, as the backward function at a fresh label's final content, the fresh label lent as fills wherever the consumed labels were (`lentK`). [Entry] of a match not in tail position (`matchEntry`): the accesses of the places the block would capture, and, after the arm and the join, for a match whose declared type is a borrow, its result's label lent as fills at the places the block takes by `&` and wherever the borrows it takes by move are lent (`entryLend`, `entrySnapshot`). The join's drop (`joinDrop`). And the strict tests: every end (`EndOk`: the holder's content a member of the label's type at every typed future), every value put at a type (`SM.strictS`), every borrow argument's label live at its parameter's content type (`BorrowArgsTyped`).

*The run in place* (`CallRef.runCallRef`, the checker's `runBody`). At the call point $s_0$, the head and the arguments the caller's last temporaries: for a callee returning a borrow, reserve the result's label $g$, lent as fills wherever the borrow arguments are; push the callee's frame (`callFrame`): its captured values, its own name bound to its meaning $G$ at its declared type, each parameter at its type, a borrow parameter $x_i$ taking over the argument's label $k_i$ (the _parameter labels_ $"ks"$, in parameter order); run the body in tail position at the codomain; pop (`popCallFrame`): end, in label order and strictly, every label the body made and every label the frame holds, but the returned one, and drop the frame; rename the returned label into $g$ (`endInto`).

*The run in the closure* (`RunCongr.fixBodyAt`, the `.fix` clause's `bodyAt`). From the closure's forming state under a fresh bottom frame (`SState.under`): bind its own name to a given meaning, then each parameter (`fixStep`): a borrow parameter gets a fresh label $l_i$, a _cell_ in frame 0 reading $l_i$'s final content at the content type and holding $l_i$'s one loan site, and the parameter holding $l_i$, with the argument as content and record; any other parameter is a plain binding. Run the body in tail position at the codomain; end, strictly, every label at or above the forming counter but the returned one; the _finals_ are the cells' meanings. The closure's function (`fixG`) is this at stage $"rank"(a) + 1$, its own name bound to stage $"rank"(a)$ at the function type weakened to `Unit` at and above that rank (`fixStage`); a function with no decreasing parameter has rank 0. `callOf` applies `closureAt` of that function.

*The renaming* σ sends the closure's labels to the in-place ones: $sigma(l_i) = k_i$, and the closure's counter onwards to the in-place counter onwards, in order. An in-place future ψ is read in the closure as $psi compose sigma$ (`renF`).

*The relation* (`FrameRel σ s₀ ks s s'`), between an in-place state $s$ and a closure state $s'$, for the call point $s_0$ and its parameter labels:
- (F1) σ is one to one and fresh-to-fresh (`inj`, `fresh`);
- (F2) in place the caller's frames of $s_0$ and one more; in the closure frame 0 and one more (`frames`);
- (F3) the caller's frames keep their stored types, readings and numbers of temporaries, and their holders (`lowerSkel`, `lowerShape`);
- (F4) each caller position reads at ψ what it read at the call at the future that gives each $k_i$ the current meaning of cell $i$ read at $psi compose sigma$ (`lowerVal`, `cellFuture`);
- (F5) the top frames hold the same bindings and temporaries in the same order, each binding with the same reading and recursion record, its stored type's label renamed, and its meaning at ψ the closure's at $psi compose sigma$ (`top`, `BindRen`); in particular the function's own binding is the same on both sides;
- (F6) the top frames' holders correspond through σ, and the closure holds nothing outside its top frame (`topShape`, `clShape`);
- (F7) every closure-live label has its image's content type, and its record unless it is a parameter label (`held`);
- (F8) the in-place held labels' sites in the top frame correspond through σ, and each such label is in σ's image; the closure lends only in its top frame or at its cells; the caller's own borrows keep their sites (`lentTop`, `lentImg`, `lentCl`, `lentCaller`);
- (F9) every closure-live label is sent to a parameter label or to a label at or above the call point's counter (`topLabels`).

*The call point* (`FrameStart`): (S1) the caller's frames read no label at or above the counter; (S2) the parameter labels are below it and distinct; (S3) the caller holds none of them, and every label it holds is below the counter; (S4) the caller's borrows are lent only in the caller's frames.

== Three changes to the model <sec-frame-model>

The frame property is a statement about the model alone. Where the model's definition differs from the checker's rule, the statement either fails or covers less than the argument uses it for. Three such places are known; each change below is a definition on paper, for the Lean lane to compare its repair against. None changes the checker.

*(M1) A function type's later parameter types and codomain are read with a cell for each borrow parameter* (the second referee's `G1`, R1). Today the `.pi` and `.fix` clauses read them (`runBinds`, `codOf`) in `extendBy`: the forming state's top frame with the earlier arguments pushed as plain bindings, a borrow parameter holding its content and no label. The checker reads them at the generic call of [Def] and [Conv-pi], where a borrow parameter holds a borrow whose loan sits in a cell. The two differ wherever a side of an `Id` in a type moves or re-points a borrow parameter, and then the model's type is weaker: `G1`'s second domain is `True` in the model and `False` in the checker, so `G1`, accepted, has no meaning.

_The definition._ Let a Π-type or a closure have parameters $x_1 : A_1, ..., x_n : A_n$ and codomain $B$, read in a state $s$ (its capture frame, under (M2)). For arguments $a_1, ..., a_(i-1)$ that are members of the domains before them, the _generic state_ $"Gen"_s (a_1, ..., a_(i-1))$ is $s$ under a fresh bottom frame with, for each $j < i$ in order: if $A_j$ is $\&A'_j$, a fresh label $l_j$ from $s$'s counter, a cell in frame 0 reading $l_j$'s final content at the content type $D_j (a_1, ..., a_(j-1))$ and holding $l_j$'s one loan site, and $x_j$ pushed on the top frame holding $l_j$, with content and record $a_j$ and content type $D_j (a_1, ..., a_(j-1))$; otherwise $x_j$ pushed holding $a_j$, with no label. Then $D_i (a_1, ..., a_(i-1))$ is the type the strict run of $A_i$ (of $A'_i$, for $\&A'_i$) returns from $"Gen"_s (a_1, ..., a_(i-1))$, and the codomain at $a_1, ..., a_n$ is the type the strict run of $B$ returns from $"Gen"_s (a_1, ..., a_n)$. This is `fixStep` without the function's own name, which is bound in no type (the paper's binding rules). Every reading of a parameter type or a codomain uses it: the strict call's decoding (`domAfter`), the body's expected type (`fixCod`), a deferred type (`codeMeaning.paramTy`) and the in-place frame's stored types (`callFrame`'s binders). With it the model's domain is the checker's generic reading, and the transfer of a statement to a call site is a corollary of the frame property (C2 below); without it that corollary is false, by `G1`.

*(M2) A closure's and a Π-type's code is read in the frame of its captured values,* as the checker's [Capture] makes them and as the model's `meanV` already reads a closure value. Today the `.fix` clause runs the body from the WHOLE forming state under a fresh bottom frame (`bodyAt` closes over the state before the captures are accessed: `s.under.pushBind …`), and `.pi` reads its domains there too; so the body's run sees every frame of the forming state, every binding of the forming frame, and every borrow live there. The checker never does: `Machine.capture` accesses each captured variable, records its value and re-indexes the code over the captures alone (`mapFree`), and [Def] checks the closure at $Gamma(Pi)";" phi(Pi)$, whose frame holds the captures and the parameters only.

_The definition._ The `.fix` clause accesses each captured variable in order (`capStep`'s access), records its meaning (which then reads no live label), stored type and reading, moves the non-copy ones as now, and means what `meanV` gives the closure value so formed: the run of the code, re-indexed as `Machine.capture` re-indexes it, from `stateOf 0` of the captures (`meanV`'s `.clo` clause, the counter at 0 as a sealed program's run has it). `.pi` likewise, with `.tPi`. `runBodyRef` reads the callee's parameter types in the same frame. What it removes: R2 (a closure formed in the body nests frames the relation cannot follow; with (M2) its meaning is a function of its captured values, and the clause is one line, @sec-frame-proof); R3 (c) (a closure's value means what the strict run formed: by definition); R3 (d) (the parameter types at the start: read in the same frame on both sides); and R7 (a) (the fixpoint equation at every forming state: a global's meaning is formed in one state, `stateOf 0 []`). Without (M2) the theorem needs the relation at a depth (@sec-frame-depth), whose proof is not done.

*(M3) Every end inside an observation is a strict point* (`theory3`'s proposal, kept): the holder's content is a whole member of its label's type at every typed future, as at every other end. The checker already rejects there (D98: no borrow ends over a moved-out content, in an observation as anywhere). It makes each cell's final a whole member at an observation, which the `Id` clause uses (@sec-frame-id).

== The statement, its hypotheses, and its uses <sec-frame-stmt>

*Theorem FR (the frame property; Lean `Steps/FrameRec.RunFrame`, open).* Assume (M2). Let the run's context meet the fundamental lemma's conditions (`FundCtx`), let the call point $s_0$ meet (S1) to (S4), let $t$'s embedded values be good (`valsOk`), and let $s$ and $s'$ be related by `FrameRel` with:
- (H1) $s$ good (`SGood`);
- (H2) $s'$ good;
- (H3) every position of $s'$ reads only labels live in $s'$, and no position of its top frame reads a parameter label (the two locality clauses);
- (H4) frame 0 of $s'$ has a binding for each parameter label;
- (H5) every holder $s$ records is a position of $s$ (`ShapeAt`);
- (H6) $t$ forms no `Id` over a parameter label: it contains no `Id`, or there is no parameter label, or (M3) holds and the call point's caller frames, resolved, tell apart the parameters' whole members (_resolved determination_, @sec-frame-id).
Then the strict runs of $t$ from $s$ and from $s'$, at the same future, assignment, tail flag and expected type, both fail, or both succeed with the same result, its label renamed by σ, and final states that are related by `FrameRel` and again satisfy (H1) to (H5).

The conclusion carries (H1) to (H5) because the induction needs them at every sub-run; Lean's `FrameAgree` concludes `FrameRel` only and will need strengthening inside its proof (R9).

*Why each hypothesis is there.* Each was forced by a refutation; each refutation is a state no call makes, so each hypothesis holds at every call (the start lemma, @sec-frame-fix) and is kept by every run (the conclusion).
- (H1), D113 (`Strict/FrameCE.run_frame_false`): the whole-state test after a write or a borrow at a field path reads every binding, in place the caller's frames, of which `FrameRel` says only how they read; a caller binding holding `err` makes the run in place fail where the closure's succeeds.
- (H2), D201 (`Strict/FrameCellCE.runFrameS_false`): the same test reads the closure's frame 0, of which `FrameRel` says only its first cells' meanings; a further binding `junk : Nat ↦ err` there makes `x.0 := c` fail in the closure and succeed in place.
- (H3), D129 (`Strict/FrameLocCE.run_frame_loc_false`): `FrameRel` compares meanings as functions of the future through σ, but a run reads a meaning at one future, which off the live labels is the run's own: the closure at $l$, in place at $sigma(l)$. One variable reading label 0 in the closure and label 5 in place, $sigma(l) = l + 5$, nothing live, reads differently at the future giving 5 the number 1.
- (H4), D203 (`Steps/FrameEnd.lowerVal_end_needs_cells`): the caller's frames are read at the cells' meanings (F4); with no cell for a parameter label, its end in place changes what a caller position reads, and nothing in the closure does.
- (H5), D209: [End] finds a label's holder by searching the positions (`holderOf`); a shape entry at no position is missed in place while the closure finds its counterpart. Bookkeeping: every run keeps it.
- (H6), D70, R6, and @sec-frame-id: an `Id` observes every position after ending every borrow, in place the caller's frames, in the closure the cells. The propositions agree only when the caller's frames, resolved, determine the cells' finals. Lean's `CellsDetermined`, which asks this of the unresolved frames at every future, is too weak (@sec-frame-id). The paper's own programs `GT` and `GT2` (case 2) are bodies with such an `Id`.
- _Not a hypothesis: the two selves._ D127 found that at a call the two frames differ at the function's own binding: in place it is $G$ at its declared type, in the closure `callOf` applies it is the stage below at the weakened type. FR compares instead with the closure run whose own binding _is_ $G$ at its true type (`fixBodyAt` at the true type; D202's route), so (F5) makes the two equal, and the difference becomes the fixpoint equation, a fact about $G$ alone (@sec-frame-fix). Lean has no separate hypothesis for it (R9).
- _Not a hypothesis: σ monotone on the callee's labels_ (D208's proposal). It is false at a state an accepted program reaches, below; what the proof needs instead is that ends of parameter labels commute (@sec-frame-inv, (I4)).

#measured[*`P4`: one access ends two parameter labels, in an order the renaming reverses.* With the `Nat` prelude, on `main` at 1b33384bf:
```
def P4 (n : Nat) (x : &Nat) (y : &Nat) : Unit := (let p : Pair(Nat, Nat) = Mk(5, 6); let r1 : &Nat = match n { Z => x, S(j) => &p.0, }; let r2 : &Nat = match n { Z => y, S(j) => &p.1, }; *r1 := 1; *r2 := 2; let z = p; ())
def UseP4a : Eq(Nat, (let a = 3; let b = 4; let ry = &a; P4(Z, &b, ry); a), 2) := refl
def UseP4b : Eq(Nat, (let a = 3; let b = 4; let ry = &a; P4(Z, &b, ry); b), 1) := refl
