All three accepted; `UseP4a` with `3` in place of `2`, and a control `Eq(Nat, Z, S(Z))`, rejected. At `n = Z` each match's [Entry] lends its result, a parameter label, at a field of `p` (`P2`'s mechanism, twice), and `let z = p` accesses `p` whole, ending both in one round, in label order. In `UseP4a` the caller made `ry`'s borrow of `a` before the borrow of `b`, so in place `y`'s label is the smaller and ends first, while in the closure `x`'s cell comes first. The two orders give the same state because neither holder reads the other's label (I4); a relation that carried monotonicity could not be started at this call.]

*The uses, as corollaries.* Each use of the frame property in @sec-twohalves is one of the following, with its own hypotheses checked.

- *(C1) A call in place* (case 1; Lean `callOf_inPlace`, open). For an accepted function value whose meaning $G$ satisfies its fixpoint equation (@sec-frame-fix), at a good call point whose borrow arguments' labels are live at their parameters' content types and whose arguments decode at the domains: the run in place succeeds exactly when `callOf` does, with the same result and the same state, the returned label renamed into the reserved one on both sides. From FR with the start lemma, the pop lemma and the fixpoint equation (@sec-frame-fix). #a-red[FR, FixEq]
- *(C2) A statement read at a call site* (case 2's transfer; [Call-type]). Assume (M1). At a call point, the strict run of a parameter type $A_i$ (or the codomain) in the frame [Call-type] pushes (the captures, and the earlier parameters bound to the arguments, a borrow parameter holding the caller's label) returns the same type as its run in $"Gen" (a_1, ..., a_(i-1))$, the arguments' meanings. This is FR for a type term (run on a copy), with the earlier borrow arguments' labels as parameter labels and no own name on either side. Its hypotheses: (S1) to (S4) and (H1) as at C1's start; (H2) because a cell reads its label's final, a whole member at every typed future, and the arguments are members of the earlier domains, by C2 at $j < i$ (an induction on $i$, the hypothesis direction of case 2); (H3), (H4), (H5) because the frame is fresh; (H6) where $A_i$ has an `Id` over an earlier borrow parameter. For a proposition, "the same type" is the same proposition, the two sides of FR's `Id` clause being equivalent. Without (M1) C2 is false: `G1`. #a-red[FR]
- *(C3) A closure's or a Π-type's value at its forming* (case 3, [T-Fix]; R3 (c)). Under (M2), by definition: the strict run's `.fix` and `.pi` mean `meanV` of the value they form, a function of the captured values' meanings, which (II) relates to the checker's captured values. Without (M2), #a-np: it is a frame property with the forming state's frames and bindings removed, the variables re-indexed and every label renamed, which the relation of FR does not express. #a-full (under M2)
- *(C4) The parameter types at the start* (the start lemma's stored types; R3 (d)). Under (M1) and (M2) the in-place frame and the closure read the parameter types in the same state, the callee's capture frame with cells: equal by definition. #a-full (under M1, M2)
- *(C5) A sealed program's re-run* (case 3). The checker re-runs a sealed program from one empty frame at its own counter; (II) relates that run to the strict run from an empty frame at the related counter; the sealed value means the strict run from `emptyState 0`. The last two agree by the renaming lemma, below. #a-red[RL]
- *(C6) The step from (II) to the theorem* (@sec-tw-concl; R1, R3, R8). Under (M1) and (M2), the generic state of [Def], read as a reference state, is `fixBodyAt`'s state for the function at the generic arguments (cells, own name the stage at the check's bound, parameters at the types $"Gen"$ gives), but for the names of the labels. So the run (II) relates to the checker's is the meaning's run, by the renaming lemma. #a-red[RL]
- *A match run directly, against the block it would close off as* (case 4; R3 (b)): not an instance. The direct run names the caller's places in the caller's frame; the block's function names its parameters in its own frame, and a by-`&` capture holds a label in the block's run that has no counterpart in the direct run. Stated as theorem N8 (@sec-frame-block).

*Theorem RL (the renaming lemma; Lean `FrameProp.run_counter_indep`, open; false as first stated, finding 7).* For bindings $b$ that read no future and carry no label, the strict runs of a term from `stateOf n b` and from `stateOf n' b` give the same result meaning and type, and states related by the shift $n' + j |-> n + j$. It is FR's case with no caller frame and no parameter label: σ is the shift, (F4) and (H4) are empty, and (H1), (H2) are not needed, because with no frame but the top one the whole-state test reads only related bindings. Its proof is FR's with the caller's-frames clauses deleted. #a-red[FR, its no-caller case]
== The invariant, replacing (P) <sec-frame-inv>

The last paper proof rested on (P), "a parameter label is lent only at its cell and in the caller's frames, never in the top frame, and is ended only singly". `P2` refutes it: after a borrow-typed match whose arm returns a borrow parameter, [Entry]'s lending lends that parameter's label, as a fill, at the places the block takes by `&`, which are in the callee's frame, and a later access of such a place ends it. What is true, and what the proof uses, is about _reads_, not sites:

- (I1) _Reads._ Every position of the closure state reads only its live labels (H3). No position of the callee's frame, on either side, reads a parameter label, and in place none reads a label the caller holds. Kept: a borrow's label is fresh and read only at the borrowed place; an end substitutes its label away everywhere; [Entry]'s lending and a call's lending add sites, not reads; a call's fixes replace each consumed label by the callee's final for it, which reads at most the call's fresh label.
- (I2) _Sites in the callee's frame._ Every live label with a site in the callee's frame is held in the callee's frame; the caller's labels have sites only in the caller's frames. Kept: a site in the callee's frame is made by a borrow, by [Entry]'s lending, or by a call's lending, each for a label held by a temporary of the callee's frame, or by `relend` at the end of a label held there, which hands that label's sites to the labels lent at its holder, a position of the callee's frame, so labels held there; and holders move only within the callee's frame until the pop.
- (I3) _Parameter labels' sites._ In place a parameter label is lent in the caller's frames and, possibly, as fills at places of the callee's frame that [Entry]'s lending added (or that `relend` moved there from such); in the closure, at its cell and at the corresponding places of the callee's frame. `FrameRel` compares the sites in the callee's frame only (F8).
- (I4) _The order of ends._ σ keeps the order of two closure labels unless both are parameter labels: on both sides the parameter labels are below the call's counter and the body's labels above it, issued in order. Two ends of parameter labels commute: neither holder reads the other's label (I1), so the two substitutions commute on meanings; `relend` commutes on sites, since lending is acyclic; and each end's strict test reads its own holder, which the other end does not change. So every fold of ends (an access's round, the pop, the ends of an observation) gives related states, whatever order σ makes of the parameter labels in it.
- (I5) The hypotheses (H1) to (H5) themselves.

`P2` and `P4` satisfy (I): their parameter labels are lent at places of the callee's frame as fills, and no meaning of that frame reads them. (I1)'s first clause is proved in Lean operation by operation (`LocalityRun.lean`: `endLabel_rinv`, `access_rinv`, `setAt_rinv`, `freshLoan_rinv`; the invariant `Locality.RInv`); the rest is argued here. #a-full

== The proof <sec-frame-proof>

By induction on $t$, as the strict run is defined (structural recursion on the term, mutual with match arms, argument lists and binders), carrying `FrameRel` and (I). (H1) and (H2) are carried by the fundamental lemma on each side (N2: a strict run keeps its state good); (H4) because no run changes frame 0's length; (H5) because a position is dropped with its shape entry (`dropLast_shape`). Each lemma below says: from related states satisfying (I), the operation succeeds on both sides or on neither, and the states after are related and satisfy (I).

*One lemma per operation on states.*

